#: src/macros/macro_rules/example.md:6
msgid "A Macros-By-Example Example"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/example.md:8
msgid ""
"Let's look at a simple declarative macro that mimics the behavior of `vec!"
"[]` for empty vector creation:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/example.md:12
msgid "// Copyright 2026 Google LLC\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/example.md:13
msgid "// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/example.md:23
msgid "\"Vector: {:?}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/example.md:27
msgid ""
"The macro is introduced with the `macro_rules!` construct, declaring a macro "
"named `my_vec`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/example.md:30
msgid ""
"**Match Arm:** The left-hand side `()` is the matcher. The pattern here "
"matches only empty inputs."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/example.md:32
msgid ""
"**Transcriber:** The right-hand side `{ Vec::new() }` is the transcriber. It "
"specifies what the macro invocation will expand to for this pattern."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/example.md:38
msgid ""
"Explain that `macro_rules!` is itself a macro, which uses a special, unique "
"language syntax for defining declarative macros."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/example.md:40
msgid ""
"Recall that although `my_vec![]` is invoked with square brackets "
"(conventional for array-like objects), any brackets (`my_vec!()` or `my_vec!"
"{}`) would also work."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/example.md:43
msgid ""
"In this case, `my_vec![]` matches empty tokens and transcribes directly to "
"`Vec::new()`."
msgstr ""
